@startuml

title A step's deadline, and the answer that arrives too late

actor "Person" as P
participant "workflow engine" as E
participant "domain service" as S
database "store" as D

P -> E : start a run
E -> D : instance in_progress, step 0 in_progress
E -> D : command_published_at = now
note right of E
  The deadline is measured from
  command_published_at, so it asks
  "how long has this dispatch been
  silent", not "how old is this run".
  The run carries the budget its
  definition stated, so a later build
  cannot expire a run started under
  a longer one.
end note
E -> S : step command

alt the service answers inside its budget
    S -> E : step completed
    E -> D : step completed
    E -> S : the next step's command
else the service is silent past its budget
    note over E
        A pass every 30s looks for a step
        whose command was published more
        than its budget ago. One pass, one
        step, one run: a step no longer
        in_progress is invisible to the next.
    end note
    E -> D : step failed\n"The step did not answer within 15m\n of its command being published (subject ...);\n it has been silent for 15m 30s. ..."
    E -> D : run failed
    note right of E
        A silent step is stopped, never
        rolled back, whatever the definition's
        failure policy declares: a step that
        reported failure did nothing, and a
        step that went silent is a step whose
        work is unknown. Rolling back a run
        whose work was in fact done would be
        the worse guess.
    end note
    E -> P : the rail reads a failed step, and why
    P -> E : retry the step
    E -> D : step in_progress again, error cleared
    E -> S : the step's command, under its own step id
    note over S
        The step id is the idempotency key, so a
        service that had already finished replays
        the outcome it holds instead of doing the
        work twice.
    end note
else the service answers after the deadline
    S -> E : step completed (late)
    E -> D : nothing: the step is no longer in_progress
    note over E
        The engine keeps the failure it recorded,
        because that is the record a person was
        shown, and it logs the arrival as an answer
        that came too late rather than as a
        duplicate. One retry advances the run.
    end note
end

@enduml
